# rule.forbid.unanswered-exits-from-a-blocker

> **a peer blocker is left by ONE door: an answer. every other exit is forbidden.**

answer = a `.taken.by_self` that states the repair or refutation, then `--as absorbed --that <slug>`.

| the exit | why not an answer | engine |
|---|---|---|
| edit the code and re-roll | absence of a re-raise is not agreement | refused |
| empty or token `.taken` | engine tests existence, not content | open |
| answer some points, not all | reviewer re-raises the unanswered | partly |
| let the reviewer exhaust | budget is not a verdict; a coast | refused |
| `--as blocked` | an unanswered concern is not a wall | open |
| call the re-raise a malfunction | a blocker that returns after your fix is a driver error: did you tell the reviewer? | open |

`--as blocked` covers a concern you have **not answered**. one you **have** answered and cannot close is a real wall: `rule.always.raise-a-blocker-a-taken-cannot-close`. sort by: *"is there a change I am PERMITTED to make that would close this point?"* yes → write the `.taken` · no → halt.

sanctioned exits: the answer; a human overrule (only after you answer what you can).

test: **"if this blocker never re-appeared, would the reviewer have been PERSUADED or merely distracted?"**

blocker: re-arrival with an unanswered blocker from the prior round · a `.taken` that names neither repair nor refutation · a re-raise called a reviewer defect with no prior answer · an overrule sought before any answer.
